an hour delay.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:22.860
So if something's happening with
the app or the system itself,

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:27.010
we can only get them
data an hour after.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:30.120
So looking at this
limitation, we actually

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.340
tried to see our
system [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:37.242
can solve our use cases for low
end to end latency of the data.

00:13:37.242 --> 00:13:38.700
And that is how,
actually, we tried

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.780
to work out in the first time.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:44.340
Our first use case is to do
stream analytics with our data.

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:48.750
So we tried to [INAUDIBLE] our
data into Kafka and Pub/Sub

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:49.980
directly.

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.190
And we built our
streaming [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:57.250
Dataflow and post data directly
into the BigQuery itself.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:01.370
And this BigQuery is going
to be [INAUDIBLE] by the BI

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:04.090
tools and the reporting
tools that we have.

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.360
So, by doing this
approach, we actually

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.590
already see a number of
[INAUDIBLE] that we can see

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.447
in the previous architecture.

00:14:11.447 --> 00:14:13.030
The first, and the
most obvious thing,

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:15.160
is the latency of
the data itself.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.900
As soon as the event comes,
it goes to Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:19.260
and BigQuery.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.930
It goes into BigQuery in
a few seconds' latency.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.152
So if something's
happening with the data--

00:14:25.152 --> 00:14:26.610
let's say you are
doing AB testing,

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.712
you are doing a
campaign-- you can

00:14:28.712 --> 00:14:30.170
actually [INAUDIBLE]
data directly,

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.430
as soon as the
event is happening.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:38.730
And the next thing
that we see from here

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:42.310
is basically the Pub/Sub,
Dataflow, and BigQuery,

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:46.362
is the first [INAUDIBLE] that
we try is try is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:46.362 --> 00:14:47.820
So you don't need
to actually worry

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.630
about installing dependencies,
installing libraries,

00:14:50.630 --> 00:14:52.790
not provisioning,
and other things

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:58.120
that you used to do when you are
using traditional technology.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:00.770
The second thing
that you can notice

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:05.296
is Dataflow has built-in
scaling feature.

00:15:05.296 --> 00:15:06.920
So you don't need to
actually hard code

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.470
your [INAUDIBLE] department
for your scaling jobs.

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.890
You can actually set the
auto-scaling feature on.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:17.190
And then your [INAUDIBLE] job
will scale up and down based

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.297
on the traffic that comes.

00:15:19.297 --> 00:15:21.630
And actually, we really reduced
the amount of operations

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:24.940
that we need to
do with our jobs.

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:28.630
And the performance of
BigQuery itself is actually

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:34.470
a lot better compared to our
SQL engine that we used before,

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:37.690
which is-- actually we
used [INAUDIBLE] before.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:40.980
So looking at this aspect
that we can already see,

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:44.950
we actually decided to broaden
our [INAUDIBLE] of Google Cloud

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:47.080
into our library system.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.840
And that is how we get to
the next stage of the data

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:55.570
infrastructure, which will look
like a number-crunching Google

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:56.890
Cloud.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:16:03.240
And this is how it really
looks like in high-level.

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.740
So data actually
will come directly

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:11.250
from the apps to our end point
that we built in Kubernetes.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.980
And it actually puts data
directly into Pub/Sub.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:18.740
It goes into Dataflow,
same as before,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.470
streaming through BigQuery.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.270
And we actually also
do another Dataflow job

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:25.960
if it going to convert
data in BigQuery

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.640
to a packet format in GCS.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.150
And we also store all
the metrics in our data

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:37.600
in Bigtable, which I am going
to talk more in details later.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.180
And all of this [INAUDIBLE]
is done through [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:47.400
And the good thing about all
of this if you are aware,

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.532
basically, is all
of this actually

00:16:49.532 --> 00:16:51.740
already have [INAUDIBLE]
with [INAUDIBLE] and logging

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:52.790
through the StackDriver.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:55.310
So you don't need to
worry about your login

00:16:55.310 --> 00:17:01.237
library or your metrics
library, such as [INAUDIBLE],, so

00:17:01.237 --> 00:17:02.070
something like that.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.940
And we also didn't need for
anything on the [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:08.940 --> 00:17:09.990
anymore.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:13.829
All of this, actually, is
either service offered by Google

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:16.373
or is deployed on the
[INAUDIBLE] in Kubernetes.

00:17:19.835 --> 00:17:21.960
The other thing here that
doesn't have auto-scaling

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.579
is basically a Bigtable.

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:28.560
And the other components, like
Kubernetes, BigQuery, Dataflow,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:29.130
Pub/Sub--

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:33.000
all of them, actually,
can scale by the demand.

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.740
So you don't need
to worry about how

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:37.230
to scale your traffic through
the BigQuery [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.190
such as end of the year,
long holiday, and so on.

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:47.400
After we get our data
into central BigQuery data

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.800
warehouse, we actually
built our data [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.981
for [INAUDIBLE] in
Traveloka to use.

00:17:52.981 --> 00:17:55.230
So from a single project at
our warehouse in BigQuery,

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:57.690
we actually built a number
of BigQuery projects,

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:01.049
as well, sitting in a different
project in Google Cloud.

00:18:01.049 --> 00:18:03.090
Why actually we are going
to do that is basically

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:05.330
we want the
permission of the data

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:08.860
marts that we have to be
as [INAUDIBLE] as possible.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:12.360
For example, let's say
I am from marketing.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:18.330
So I have data of my
as revenue, and so on.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.060
And I don't want this data to
be shared along the company.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:23.910
So I can actually choose the
data to be private to my team.

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:28.095
And I can share the data that I
want to share with other teams

00:18:28.095 --> 00:18:29.610
at Traveloka.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:32.490
So we actually have one
project for BigQuery

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:33.790
for each team in Traveloka.

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:37.350
So effectively they can
independent to choose

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.780
what data they want to share
with the company and what

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:43.470
data they want to keep
private in their team.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:46.670
And another is
basically why we have

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:50.580
data in BigQuery and
GCS, the same data.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.880
The thing is basically when
we are doing ETL from the data

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.880
warehouse to the data
marts, we are doing

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:58.050
ETL with a number of ways.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.080
Most of it is by doing
BigQuery SQL directly

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:05.370
for a simple transformation
like filter, join, and grouping.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:11.750
And under more complex ETL, we
need to use Dataflow or Spark--

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:14.200
for example, for machine
learning, and so on.

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:16.620
And if we are doing
[INAUDIBLE] with Spark,

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:18.750
actually, if Spark
can [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:20.887
directly from BigQuery,
but their efficiency

00:19:20.887 --> 00:19:23.220
for [INAUDIBLE] in BigQuery
compared to [INAUDIBLE] data

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:26.490
in [INAUDIBLE] is
still unmatchable.

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:30.300
So Spark [INAUDIBLE]
data from [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:33.090
is still much faster
compared to reading data

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:34.980
in BigQuery for now, actually.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:41.130
And all of this data
[INAUDIBLE] in BigQuery

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:43.550
is going to mirror
directly [INAUDIBLE]..

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:46.770
So every analyst in the
team, they can actually

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.300
make their own desk bot.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:52.200
And from the date mart
they can actually directly

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:55.370
query the data from the
BigQuery to their BI desk

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:57.780
bot and [? mech ?] desk bot
for the business [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.321
to see.

00:20:00.321 --> 00:20:02.070
And this is basically
the high level thing

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:04.620
that we want to share with
you [INAUDIBLE] architecture.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:06.000
The next thing that we're
going to share with you

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.180
is basically one of the
use cases of the data

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:09.500
platform in Traveloka.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:16.947
And what we call it
the [INAUDIBLE] metrics

00:20:16.947 --> 00:20:19.030
in Traveloka that we want
to share with you-- some

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:20.060
of the metrics.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.870
For example, like number of
data that we get per day--

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:24.320
a few terabytes per day.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.590
And then a number of batch
jobs that we do every day--

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:29.120
60,000-plus.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.900
The number of tasks that is
generated from the BigQuery.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:35.480
And most of these tasks
using BigQuery actually

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:37.500
is coming on a near
real-time basis.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:39.380
So even as the event
is coming, as soon

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:42.650
as booking is happening, you can
actually see the charts updated

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:45.840
at real time.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.890
And the next thing is basically
the customer data platform

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:52.730
is one of the use cases that we
want to share with you on how

00:20:52.730 --> 00:20:54.700
we do analytics in Traveloka.

00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:59.980
This is one of the
example questions

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:03.530
that the business [INAUDIBLE]
or other teams asking our team.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:05.320
For example, when did
a specific customer

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:07.150
buy from us the first time?

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:11.660
What is this guy last
purchase from us, and so on.

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:14.620
And if you see these
kinds of questions,

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:19.060
you can actually answer this
question easily using BigQuery.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:21.280
You have all the data
in your BigQuery.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:22.510
You just query your data.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:25.030
You do your select
star from the Bigtable.

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:28.740
You sort by the user ID,
and you get your answer.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:31.300
But the problem is
basically BigQuery can only

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:34.000
do a partitioning by bit.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:37.330
And it is one of the
downsides of BigQuery.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.230
And if you are are doing
these kinds of questions,

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:45.960
you don't know the day or
time stamp of the purchase.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.730
So you need to scan
all the time range

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:52.430
to filter for this
customer, and see when is

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:55.070
the purchase of this customer.

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:57.890
So you will actually incur
a lot of unnecessary scans

00:21:57.890 --> 00:22:00.150
of the data.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.070
And another thing is
basically BigQuery

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.980
is designed to be an
analytical data warehouse.

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:07.560
So it's designed to
do [INAUDIBLE] scan.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:09.210
And the response
time is supposed

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:14.640
to be a few seconds, or maybe
a minute for huge joins.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:17.190
For some use cases,
we need these answers

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:20.250
to be answered in milliseconds,
because this is not

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:21.630
going to be caught by people.

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.550
This isn't going to
be called by machine.

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.890
We [INAUDIBLE] an API
so that other people

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:30.240
actually can call the API
and get an answer directly.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:36.930
For example, and this is how we
actually solved our use cases,

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:41.610
we actually posted our data
not only in BigQuery but also

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.320
put our data in
Bigtable, as well.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:49.110
So we developed our
API in Kubernetes

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:52.650
and exposed it to our
teams in Traveloka.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:55.380
So, for example, for our
flight teams, hotels team,

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:57.090
accommodation, they
can call our API,

00:22:57.090 --> 00:23:01.424
and our API will actually
query Bigtable or BigQuery

00:23:01.424 --> 00:23:02.590
to get the answers for them.

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:09.996
And why, actually, we use
Bigtable for our use cases

00:23:09.996 --> 00:23:11.582
is because a few reasons.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.280
The first thing is
basically we want everybody

00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.360
to have [INAUDIBLE] scans.

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:24.510
This is actually one reason why
we cannot use something like

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.600
Apache Cassandra.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:29.990
As far as I know, it can
only do a simple key value

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:35.410
fetching, while we actually need
much more capability than that.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:40.500
The other thing is we need to be
able to cater dynamic columns.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:44.880
And if possible, we want it
to be many [? surface. ?]

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:47.130
Just Bigtable can
offer it to us.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:50.420
And it's also familiar
with [INAUDIBLE] API,

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:54.960
which is open source [INAUDIBLE]
API from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:59.220
And many people are already
familiar with that kind of API.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:03.150
So we are going to show
you about example data

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.250
modeling in Bigtable
that we do to answer

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.490
that kind of questions.

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:12.410
For example, these are part
of tables in our Bigtable.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.610
And we went to assess when
this specific customer bought

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:17.540
from us the first time.

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:22.830
So we actually stored the data
in this kind of data modeling.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:25.100
We actually used the
user ID as the prefix.

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.850
And we append that with the
time stamp of the event.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:30.280
So the event can be [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:33.926
For this kind of table,
the event is purchase,

00:24:33.926 --> 00:24:35.800
and the value is
[INAUDIBLE] of the purchase,

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.300
whether he bought a flight
ticket from us, or a hotel

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:41.180
voucher, or anything.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:43.360
And BigQuery is
similar like Essbase.

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.770
Sorry, Bigtable.

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:47.690
They store the data
in a sorted manner.

00:24:47.690 --> 00:24:50.540
They do not hash the
key to store the data,

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:53.370
unlike if you compare it
with Apache Cassandra.

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:57.500
So you're going to
get your data stored

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.970
something similar like this.

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:03.740
In this case, we have three
user IDs, A, B, and C.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:07.400
And the number behind is
the time stamp of the event.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:12.100
For you your data will be stored
like this, in this exact order.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:16.910
It will be the user ID, time
stamp, and the purchase.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:19.750
So if you have a
data model like this

00:25:19.750 --> 00:25:22.390
and you have a
question on I want

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:26.300
to know when is this guy
buy from us the first time.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:28.940
So what we can do is basically
we cannot do a simple key value

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:30.940
fetching, because we do
not know the time stamp.

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:32.500
What we can do is
basically we can

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:34.324
define our [INAUDIBLE]
scans for table.

00:25:34.324 --> 00:25:35.740
We can define our
[INAUDIBLE] scan

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.392
to be the user ID, because
we know the user ID,

00:25:38.392 --> 00:25:39.600
but we don't know time stamp.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:43.190
So we just put zero as a
time stamp, for example.

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:45.010
And when you are
doing ranged scans

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.840
we will be to fetch
the first row.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:48.950
We just have the
minimum time stamp.

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:50.620
And you got your alphabetically.

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:54.520
We just do first time stamp that
this customer bought something

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.280
from us.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:01.130
Another similar
problem is basically

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.160
when is the last time a specific
customer bought from us.

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:09.460
If you have this kind of
same data model, with Essbase

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:11.970
you can do a reverse
scan from the bottom.

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.730
Specify your user ID,
and theoretically you

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.380
get the last time stamp.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.790
But Bigtable actually
cannot do that.

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:22.800
While Bigtable is actually
compatible with API,

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.130
there is some small things
that Bigtable cannot support.

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:28.290
One of them is the reverse scan.

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:31.020
So we need to actually
do a small work-around

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:33.980
to be able to answer
these questions.

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.570
And this is how we solved
this kind of problem.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.630
So we altered the key.

00:26:41.630 --> 00:26:45.500
Instead of using the user ID
and the actual time stamp,

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:48.590
we actually used the user
ID and the time stamp.

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:51.320
We changed it with
the maximum time stamp

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:54.050
minus the time stamp itself.

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:56.780
If you do that way
for all your keys,

00:26:56.780 --> 00:26:59.140
you're going to end
up with your data,

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:02.972
but sorted in descending order.

00:27:02.972 --> 00:27:05.890
The first row will
be the last purchase.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:09.130
The last row will be
the first purchase.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:11.820
And if you already have
this kind of data model,

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:13.870
you can actually do
the same [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:18.120
You could do array
scans, specify your ID,

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:21.120
and you get your first
row as the last purchase

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:25.221
of the people and its details.

00:27:25.221 --> 00:27:26.970
And this is one of the
example [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:28.660
that we do in Bigtable.

00:27:28.660 --> 00:27:35.520
And we've got a few more use
cases that we do with Bigtable.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:38.220
But we're not going
to share it today.

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:41.310
And the next thing that we're
going to show you Traveloka use

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:46.320
cases is about the data
quality and anomaly framework

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:48.850
that we did in Traveloka.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:54.190
So what is the data
quality itself?

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.330
Why do we need data quality?

00:27:56.330 --> 00:27:58.600
In Traveloka, like
I mentioned before,

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:02.020
Traveloka is a company with
multiple lines of business.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:05.560
So the data actually is coming
not only from one source.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:09.170
It's coming from multiple
sources and multiple streams.

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.550
So if everything
is going smooth,

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:15.409
data is coming direct
to us in correct data.

00:28:15.409 --> 00:28:16.950
But if something is
happening-- let's

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.540
say someone actually
deploys a new version.

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:22.600
Someone [INAUDIBLE]
an app, and so on.

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:25.800
And they forgot to put the
code in for the tracking,

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.430
and the data stops coming.

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:30.520
Or the data are coming, but
it's [INAUDIBLE],, and so on.

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:35.350
And all of this actually
can cause many problems.

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:38.230
For example, your
[INAUDIBLE] process

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:40.870
can actually fail at all.

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:42.340
You cannot see your reports.

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:44.680
You will see on your desk bot.

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:47.920
Or it's still a success,
but your reports, the number

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:49.187
will be obviously wrong.

00:28:49.187 --> 00:28:51.520
And most dangerous one is
basically you don't know that,

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:53.350
but that is happening.

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:56.574
You trust your desk bot.

00:28:56.574 --> 00:28:57.990
You believe the
number is correct,

00:28:57.990 --> 00:28:59.140
but actually it's wrong.

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:05.400
And actually you are using the
wrong data to make decisions.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:07.700
And how do we solve
this problem, actually?

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:17.440
Because we have all our data
in BigQuery, all of them

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:21.084
is mostly structured data.

00:29:21.084 --> 00:29:22.750
There are tables, and
they have columns,

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:26.140
and each column has
their own data type.

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:29.430
For example, for this table
we have a few columns.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:32.470
First of all, we have a string
column and an integer column.

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:36.190
And each of the columns
itself it has its own metrics.

00:29:36.190 --> 00:29:38.440
For example, the string column--

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:41.140
we actually can
actually know what

00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:43.430
is the proportion of naught
value of this column.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:46.000
What is the distinct
value of this column,

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.050
and other metrics as well.

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:51.930
If you actually collect
all these metrics for each

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:55.470
of the columns and you
collect it over time,

00:29:55.470 --> 00:30:00.580
you will end up with a number
of time series for a table.

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:04.980
So for one table, you can
develop a number [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.972
The obvious thing is
number of rows of tables.

00:30:07.972 --> 00:30:09.930
The next thing is basically
each of the columns

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:11.800
has its own metrics.

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:15.210
For sample, [INAUDIBLE] column,
for example like the country--

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:18.570
you have the number
of distinct countries.

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:22.510
You have actually a number
of null countries, and so on.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:26.960
So if you already have all
these metrics from your tables,

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:31.080
actually refer to these metrics
to determine whether your table

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.030
is correct or not.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:38.230
For example, when you have
these kind of metrics,

00:30:38.230 --> 00:30:39.946
you can actually detect--

00:30:39.946 --> 00:30:42.070
let's say that your data
has stopped coming at all.

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:43.778
You're going to notice
the number of rows

00:30:43.778 --> 00:30:45.000
actually is dropping.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:48.610
Or let's say someone is
[INAUDIBLE] your mobile apps.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:53.330
And it forgot to put
some code in the country.

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:55.180
So all your country
will become null.

00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:58.625
But when I say in normal days
you have 10 countries coming

00:30:58.625 --> 00:30:59.500
to [INAUDIBLE] data--

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:03.020
you have Singapore, Indonesia,
Malaysia, and so on.

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:05.770
And then suddenly one day
you only have two countries.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:09.120
And you know something
is happening.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:12.215
And maybe the number is
wrong, and some other thing

00:31:12.215 --> 00:31:13.130
is going on.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:17.010
So by having all this
metadata of your data,

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:20.480
you can actually analyze your
time series off your table.

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:24.480
And it's reminding
where your data is from.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.200
And this is actually how
we built our approach

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:30.410
to solve this.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:33.170
This is the same BigQuery
that we used before.

00:31:33.170 --> 00:31:35.600
It contains all the raw data.

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:39.310
And we [INAUDIBLE] the
performance of the BigQuery

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:43.310
to gather the data metrics
from BigQuery itself.

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:45.940
So we don't need to actually
spin up another cluster--

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:49.250
[INAUDIBLE] cluster, another
[INAUDIBLE] cluster to actually

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:50.960
do the heavy lifting for us.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:54.260
We do gather all the
queries using the BigQuery

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:57.620
SQL [INAUDIBLE],, store
it in the Bigtable,

00:31:57.620 --> 00:32:02.280
and use it for the
[INAUDIBLE] analysis.

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:04.770
And after you have
[INAUDIBLE] this,

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:07.380
you can actually
do your [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:11.110
on a time series using single
approach or a few approach.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:15.540
For example, you can actually
do a [INAUDIBLE] mean,

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:16.800
standard deviation.

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:21.240
Or you can use a library
from Facebook [INAUDIBLE],,

00:32:21.240 --> 00:32:23.580
actually is a time
series analysis

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:26.190
in Python from Facebook.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:28.470
And some other library
that you can use.

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:30.450
By using this kind of
[INAUDIBLE] analysis,

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:33.330
you can actually analyze
whether your specific table,

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:37.780
or specific column in your
table, are behaving wrongly.

00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:42.500
While actually this is not going
to cover all the use cases,

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.760
it's actually going to
cover most of the use cases

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:46.800
that we encounter.

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:50.464
And it's actually
helping us a lot.

00:32:50.464 --> 00:32:51.880
The impact that
we can already see

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:56.420
is basically we can see the data
is wrong as soon as possible.

00:32:56.420 --> 00:33:00.530
Previously, if data is wrong,
we don't know what is happening.

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:03.450
We know data is wrong
from the stakeholder.

00:33:03.450 --> 00:33:06.310
Stakeholder, they see the desk
bot, they see the BI tools,

00:33:06.310 --> 00:33:08.950
they see the emails, and the see
the number is obviously wrong.

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:10.630
Or the number is
not showing at all.

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:13.565
And they tell our
team, hey, can you

00:33:13.565 --> 00:33:17.360
check something with the system
if something is happening?

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:19.090
So by implementing
this kind of system,

00:33:19.090 --> 00:33:23.420
we actually know the data is
wrong as soon as possible.

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:28.840
And we can actually inform the
downstream user of the data

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:32.740
where do we have a data problem,
or whether it's a false alarm,

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:34.850
or something is happening.

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:38.500
And it's going to make your
trustworthiness for your team,

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:41.320
because people can actually
trust the responses coming

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:44.320
from your team much better
than the previous one.

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:47.780
They can get the
reports, but they do not

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:51.610
trust whether this
number is correct or not.

00:33:51.610 --> 00:33:53.740
So this is one of
the use cases that we

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:55.075
[INAUDIBLE] in Traveloka.

00:33:55.075 --> 00:33:56.950
And the next thing we're
going to share, too,

00:33:56.950 --> 00:34:01.330
is the lessons learned when we
implemented all these things.

00:34:06.769 --> 00:34:07.810
The first thing is this--

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:11.280
BigQuery has great performance,
especially if you are doing

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:13.469
SQL analysis with raw data.

00:34:13.469 --> 00:34:17.969
For most use cases, especially
for most BI use cases,

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:22.199
actually you can solve
within using SQL,

00:34:22.199 --> 00:34:25.170
mostly using group by,
join, filter, and so on.

00:34:29.530 --> 00:34:34.070
For auto-scaling feature in
Dataflow and under components

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:36.830
such as Pub/Sub,
BigQuery, Kubernetes,

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:38.690
it's really useful to us.

00:34:38.690 --> 00:34:41.389
You don't need to worry
about your big period,

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:42.389
your end of the year--

00:34:42.389 --> 00:34:44.388
you need to scale up your
[INAUDIBLE] and so on.

00:34:44.388 --> 00:34:46.949
It's all in the past.

00:34:46.949 --> 00:34:51.830
And all of this [INAUDIBLE]
in Google Cloud, like Pub/Sub,

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:54.690
Kubernetes, and
technology itself, they

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:56.440
have all the
built-in [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:56.440 --> 00:34:57.630
to the stack driver.

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:00.640
So you have all your metrics
flowing in automatically.

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:02.670
You don't need to worry
about implementing

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:06.770
your own [INAUDIBLE]
system or your own logging,

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:10.020
using [INAUDIBLE] into
some other system.

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:12.390
All of them is going to be
available for you directly.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.900
So you can actually
do a debugging

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:17.670
if something is happening
to your application.

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:20.790
You can monitor your CPU
needs, your query in BigQuery

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:26.890
if something is running
slow and something else.

00:35:26.890 --> 00:35:32.930
And I think this is more or
less the whole thing that I

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:34.730
want to share with you today.

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:39.880
And I'm going to give
it back to Gaurav.

00:35:39.880 --> 00:35:40.490
Thanks.

00:35:40.490 --> 00:35:41.198
GAURAV ANAND: OK.

00:35:41.198 --> 00:35:42.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:47.140
